      "finalExcerpt": "I fixed the bug, and all 5 tests pass (the 4 that were already there plus the new regression test).\n\n`parse_price` in `inventory.py` now treats commas as thousands separators when the string matches one of two patterns:\n- groups of exactly three digits followed by a dot, as in `\"1,234.50\"`\n- two or more comma groups, as in `\"1,234,567\"`\n\nIn those cases it strips the commas. Anything else with a comma is still read as a European decimal comma, so `\"12,50\"` still gives `12.5`. `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` also still work, and `total_value` is unchanged.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` is ambiguous, and the issue doesn't cover it. It still parses as `1.234`, the decimal-comma reading.\n\nI added `test_thousands_separator` to `tests/test_inventory.py`. It covers `\"1,234.50\"`, `\"$1,234,567.89\"` and `\"1,234,567\"`.",
