      "finalExcerpt": "I fixed the bug, and all 5 tests pass (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`).\n\n`parse_price` in `inventory.py` now treats commas as thousands separators when the whole string looks like `1,234.50` or `1,234,567`. That means groups of exactly three digits after the commas, followed by a dot or another comma group. Everything else still goes through the old decimal-comma path, so `\"12,50\"`, `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` behave as before. `total_value` is unchanged.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` (one comma group, no dot) is still read as a European decimal, giving `1.234`. The issue only calls a comma a thousands separator when a dot or another comma group follows, so I left that case alone.\n\nI added a regression test, `test_thousands_separator`, in `tests/test_inventory.py`. It covers `\"1,234.50\"` → `1234.5` and `\"$1,234,567\"` → `1234567.0`.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
