      "finalExcerpt": "I fixed the bug, and all 6 tests pass. I ran them before the fix and the two new thousands-separator tests failed. After the fix they pass, along with the 4 existing tests.\n\n**Cause:** `parse_price` in `inventory.py` treated every comma as a European decimal comma. `\"1,234.50\"` therefore became `\"1.\n…\n and either has a dot or has more than one comma. `\"1,234.50\"` gives `1234.5`, and `\"$1,234,567.89\"` and `\"1,234,567\"` also parse correctly. Everything else still goes through the old comma-to-dot path, so `\"12,50\"` still gives `12.5`. I didn't touch `total_value`.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` (one comma, three digits, no dot) still parses as the decimal `1.234`, because the issue only calls a comma a thousands separator when a dot or another comma group follows.\n\n**Tests:** I added two regression tests in `tests/test_inventory.py`, for `\"1,234.50\"` and for multiple groups.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
