      "finalExcerpt": "`parse_price(\"1,234.50\")` now returns `1234.5`, and all 5 tests pass, including the new regression test.\n\nThe fix is in `inventory.py`. A comma followed by exactly three digits and then a dot or another comma is now treated as a thousands separator and removed. `\"12.50\"`, `\"$3\"` and `\"12,50\"` behave as before, and I didn't touch `total_value`. The regression test in `tests/test_inventory.py` covers `\"1,234.50\"` and `\"$1,234,567.25\"`.\n\nI skipped the bare `\"1,234\"` case, since the issue doesn't say whether it means 1234 or 1.234. It still parses as a decimal comma, giving `1.234`. That follows the issue's rule, but it could surprise someone who means a thousands separator.",
